[31.129201, "o", "FINDING: HTTP/HTTPS Traffic Blocking Issue\r\n\r\nAFFECTED RULES:\r\n- Rule 7: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m string --string \"Mozilla\" --algo bm -j ACCEPT\r\n- Rule 8: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -m string --string \"Mozilla\" --algo bm -j ACCEPT\r\n- Rule 9: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP\r\n- Rule 10: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j DROP\r\n\r\nROOT CAUSE:\r\nThe iptables ruleset implements a flawed User-Agent filtering mechanism. Rules 7-8 only accept HTTP/HTTPS traffic if the packet contains the string \"Mozilla\" (attempting to filter by browser type). Rules 9-10 then explicitly DROP all other HTTP/HTTPS traffic that doesn't match.\r\n\r\nWHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:\r\n- SSH (port 22): Has an unconditional ACCEPT rule with no string matching requirements\r\n- HTTP/HTTPS (ports 80/443): Require packets to contain \"Mozilla\" string to be accepted\r\n\r\nWHY CURL HANGS:\r\nThe curl command sends a User-Agent header like \"curl/7.x.x\" which does not contain the string \"Mozilla\". Therefore:\r\n1. The packet doesn't match rules 7-8 (no \"Mozil"]
[31.129302, "o", "la\" string found)\r\n2. The packet hits rules 9-10 and gets DROPped (not REJECTed)\r\n3. Since DROP silently discards packets, curl waits indefinitely for a response that never comes\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATION:\r\nThis string-matching approach for access control is fundamentally flawed:\r\n- It can be trivially bypassed by adding \"Mozilla\" to any User-Agent\r\n- It blocks legitimate tools (curl, wget, scripts, monitoring systems, APIs)\r\n- String matching on packet payload is CPU-intensive and unreliable\r\n- It provides no real security benefit\r\n\r\nThe organization should remove rules 7-10 and implement proper access controls using:\r\n- Source IP whitelisting (-s flag)\r\n- Network-based restrictions (firewall zones)\r\n- Application-layer authentication (not firewall rules)\r\n"]
